Could improve interface with overloads rather than casts, but fake compile maybe. Let's try interface overloads:
```
on(event:'metrics', listener:(buffer: Buffer)=>void):unknown;
...
```
Fake test `on(event:string, listener:(...args:unknown[])=>void)` signature implementing interface with overloads likely fails "not assignable". We can define CaptureSdk on generic event map:
```
export interface CaptureSdkEventMap {
 metrics: [buffer: Buffer];
 validationStatus: [code:number,timestampUs:number,hint: unknown];
 videoOutput:[buffer:Buffer,width:number,height:number,stride:number];
 error:[code:number,message:string,retryable:boolean];
 processingStatus:[status:ProcessingStatus];
}
export interface CaptureSdk {
 on<K extends keyof CaptureSdkEventMap>(event: K, listener:(...args:CaptureSdkEventMap[K])=>void):unknown;
 ...
}
```
Fake implementation with generic? `on<K extends keyof...>(event:K, listener:(...args:EventMap[K])=>void):this` works. `emit` test methods can generic too. More code but type-safe. Use ProcessingStatus type imported runtime. Buffer type from node.

Production calls infer typed callbacks. Fake test needs implement generic. This is clean. Let's modify interface and fake. `CaptureSdkEventMap` export maybe test import for emit? Fake can define its own method with methods signatures explicitly or use generic map imported.
Fake:
```
private listeners = new Map<string, Array<(...args: never[]) => void>>() // no
```
Simpler FakeSdk not implement interface, cast `const sdk = new FakeSdk() as unknown as CaptureSdk`, but test startCapture input expects CaptureSdk. Then emit uses any-ish arrays. We want TS strict.
Define Fake with generic map:
```
private listeners = new Map<keyof CaptureSdkEventMap, Array<(...args: unknown[])=>void>>()
on<K extends keyof...>(event:K, listener:(...args:CaptureSdkEventMap[K])=>void): this {
